 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are pushed to browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the delivery component publishes each committed event revision to connected socket sessions, and browsers that miss revisions while disconnected catch up by replay from the change store.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+This couples three nodes that must be designed together: the change store keeps replayable revisions and a publication path the delivery component can rely on; the delivery component owns socket session management and reliable publication; and browser sync owns reconnect and catch-up on top of the socket. The project operates its own socket infrastructure — connection capacity and reconnect handling are accepted operational work, in exchange for fast updates without a realtime vendor dependency.
 
